"author"><name><surname>Ukhanov</surname><given-names>K.</given-names></name><name><surname>Payne</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Rapid coupling of calcium release to depolarization in <italic>Limulus</italic> polyphemus ventral photoreceptors as revealed by microphotolysis and confocal microscopy</article-title><source>J. Neurosci.</source><volume>17</volume><year>1997</year><fpage>1701</fpage><lpage>1709</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9030629</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Ukhanovetal1995"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ukhanov</surname><given-names>K.Y.</given-names></name><name><surname>Flores</surname><given-names>T.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hsiao</surname><given-names>H.S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Mohapatra</surname><given-names>P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Pitts</surname><given-names>C.H.</given-names></name><name><surname>Payne</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Measurement of cytosolic Ca<sup>2+</sup> concentration in <italic>Limulus</italic> ventral photoreceptors using fluorescent dyes</article-title><source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source><volume>105</volume><year>1995</year><fpage>95</fpage><lpage>116</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7730791</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Walzetal1995"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Walz</surname><given-names>B.</given-names></name><name><surname>Baumann</surname><given-names>O.</given-names></name><name><surname>Zimmermann</surname><given-names>B.</given-names></name><name><surname>Ciriacy-Wantrup</surname><given-names>E.V.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Caffeine- and ryanodine-sensitive Ca(2+)-induced Ca<sup>2+</sup> release from the endoplasmic reticulum in honeybee photoreceptors</article-title><source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source><volume>105</volume><year>1995</year><fpage>537</fpage><lpage>567</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7608657</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="YeandleandSpiegler1973"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yeandle</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Spiegler</surname><given-names>J.B.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Light-evoked and spontaneous discrete waves in the ventral nerve photoreceptor of <italic>Limulus</italic></article-title><source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source><volume>61</volume><year>1973</year><fpage>552</fpage><lpage>571</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">4705637</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="YeandleandSrebro1970"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yeandle</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Srebro</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Latency fluctuations of discrete waves in the <italic>Limulus</italic> photoreceptor</article-title><source>J. Opt. Soc. Am.</source><volume>60</volume><year>1970</year><fpage>398</fpage><lpage>401</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">5437485</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn><p><italic>Abbreviations used in this paper:</italic> ASW, artificial sea water&#x0003b; CPA, cyclopiazonic acid&#x0003b; ER, endoplasmic reticulum&#x0003b; InsP<sub>3</sub>, inositol (1,4,5) trisphosphate&#x0003b; R-lobe, rhabdomeral lobe.</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-wrap><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>(A) Superimposed receptor potentials recorded after five pairs of dim and then bright flashes and after one dim flash alone. The flashes were delivered at the times indicated by the stimulus monitors below the membrane potential traces. For each pair, a dim flash (5-ms duration, delivering &#x0223c;500 effective photons) was followed after a variable interval by a bright flash (1&#x02013;5), delivered by the unattenuated laser (30-ms duration). After each bright flash, the receptor potentials departed after a latent period from the response to the dim flash alone and saturated at 50 mV. (B) Relative fluorescence, &#x00394;F/F<sub>rest</sub> of Oregon green-5N (clusters of identical symbols) recorded during the latent period of the responses to the five bright flashes.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8155.f1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Receptor potentials (solid lines) and Oregon green-5N fluorescence, (symbols) recorded after flashes that delivered 5,000 (A), 200 (B), and 50 (C) effective photons. Dye fluorescence is shown as the ratio of the fluorescence increase that followed a dim flash, &#x00394;F, to the peak fluorescence increase, &#x00394;F<sub>max</sub>, observed during a step of unattenuated laser illumination delivered to the same confocal spot. Bars below each set of traces indicate the timing of the dim flashes, 5-, 20-, and 50-ms duration and 2, 4, and 5 log<sub>10</sub> U attenuation, respectively.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8155.f2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>(A) The light-induced increase in Oregon green-5N fluorescence at the peak of each response (mean &#x000b1; SEM) is plotted against the flash energy. The fluorescence increase, &#x00394;F, is shown relative to the peak fluorescence increase, &#x00394;F<sub>max</sub>, observed during a step of unattenuated laser illumination to the same confocal spot. (B) Light-induced increases in intracellular free calcium concentration, Ca<sub>i,</sub> (mean &#x000b1; SEM), calculated from the data of B using <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="FD3"/>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8155.f3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Rising phases of receptor potentials (solid lines) and reconstructed elevations of Ca<sub>i</sub> (&#x025cb;) recorded from four different cells after flashes that delivered 50,000, 5,000, 2,000, and 200 effective photons. The flashes, 5-, 5-, 20-, and 20-ms duration&#x0003b; 1, 2, 3, and 4 log<sub>10</sub> U attenuation, respectively, were delivered at the origin of the time axis.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8155.f4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>(A) The times taken for receptor potentials (T<sub>c, mV</sub>&#x0003b; &#x025b4;) and reconstructed elevations of Ca<sub>i</sub> (T<sub>c, Ca</sub><sup>2+</sup>&#x0003b; &#x025cb;) recorded from 13 cells filled with Oregon green-5N to exceed 8 mV or 2 &#x003bc;M, respectively, are plotted against the flash energy. (B) For each flash response used to create A, T<sub>c, mV</sub> is plotted against T<sub>c, Ca</sub><sup>2+</sup>. The solid straight line represents <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="FD5"/> in the text, with &#x00394;<italic>t</italic> = 2.2 ms.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8155.f5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>(A) Receptor potential (solid line) and reconstructed elevation of Ca<sub>i</sub> (symbols) recorded after a flash that delivered &#x0223c;50 effective photons to a photoreceptor filled with fluo-4. The bar beneath the trace indicates the timing of the flash, 50-ms duration, 5 log<sub>10</sub> U attenuation. (B) The rising edge of the response in A is shown on an expanded time scale, together with an estimate of Ca<sub>i</sub> and a receptor potential recorded during a step of illumination by the unattenuated laser beam, delivering &#x0223c;10<sup>8</sup> effective photons/s. The bars below the traces indicate the onset and duration of the stimuli.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8155.f6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Receptor potentials (lines) and estimated elevations of Ca<sub>i</sub> (&#x02022;) recorded from a chemically bleached photoreceptor filled with fluo-4 during five successive continuous steps of illumination from the unattenuated laser beam, delivered at the origin of the time axis (bar below traces). The latencies of the signals vary on either side of the vertical line from flash to flash.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8155.f7"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F8"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>(A) For each of the five responses shown in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F7">Fig. 7</xref>, the times, T<sub>c, mV</sub>, taken for receptor potentials to exceed 8 mV are plotted against the times, T<sub>c, Ca</sub><sup>2+</sup>, taken for the elevation of Ca<sub>i</sub> to exceed 1 &#x003bc;M. The solid straight line represents <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="FD5"/> in the text, with &#x00394;<italic>t</italic> = 1.4 ms. (B) To compare fluctuations in the latencies of receptor potentials and elevations of Ca<sub>i</sub> measured in five cells filled with fluo-4, T<sub>c, mV</sub> and T<sub>c, Ca2+</sub> for individual responses were subtracted from their means for each cell, and the resulting deviation scores were plotted against one another. The solid straight line indicates equality.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8155.f8"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F9"><label>Figure 9</label><caption><p>Superimposed elevations of Ca<sub>i</sub> and receptor potentials (top and bottom traces, respectively, in each panel) during five successive steps of illumination delivered from the unattenuated laser beam to two cells, A and B, filled with fluo-4. To compensate for the variable latency of each response, the value of T<sub>c, mV</sub> was determined for each response and was subtracted from the time base of each response. The arrows show the times when the light-induced increase of Ca<sub>i</sub> was detectable, indicating the variation in the timing of the Ca<sup>2+</sup> signal relative to that of the electrical response for these cells.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8155.f9"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F10"><label>Figure 10</label><caption><p>(A) Representative receptor potentials (solid lines) and reconstructed elevation of Ca<sub>i</sub> (symbols) recorded from a chemically bleached photoreceptor filled with fluo-4 after a flash that delivered &#x0223c;10 effective photons. The bar beneath the traces indicates the timing of the flash, 40-ms duration, 3 log<sub>10</sub> U attenuation. (B) The rising edge of the responses are shown on an expanded time scale, together with an estimate of Ca<sub>i</sub> and a receptor potential recorded during a step of illumination by the unattenuated laser beam, delivering &#x0223c;10<sup>5</sup> effective photons/s. The bars below the traces indicate the onset and duration of the stimuli. Note the large fluctuations in the amplitude and latency of the representative receptor potentials after the dim flash.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8155.f10"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F11"><label>Figure 11</label><caption><p>(A and B) Membrane current (solid lines&#x0003b; inward current is shown as an upward deflection) and estimated elevation of Ca<sub>i</sub> (&#x025cb;) recorded from a chemically bleached photoreceptor filled with fluo-4 that was voltage clamped to its resting potential (&#x02212;55 mV) during steps of illumination by the unattenuated laser beam, delivering &#x0223c;10<sup>5</sup> effective photons/s (bars below traces), recorded when the cell was bathed in ASW (A) and 15 min after switching to 0 Ca-ASW (B). (C) Representative receptor potential (solid line) and reconstructed elevation of Ca<sub>i</sub> (symbols) recorded from a photoreceptor filled with Oregon green-5N that was bathed for 15 min in 0 Ca-ASW after a flash that delivered &#x0223c;200 effective photons. The flash (bar beneath trace), 20-ms duration, 4 log<sub>10</sub> U attenuation, was delivered at the origin of the time axis.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8155.f11"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F12"><label>Figure 12</label><caption><p>Receptor potentials (solid lines) and elevations of Ca<sub>i</sub> (&#x025cb;) recorded from a chemically bleached photoreceptor filled with fluo-4 during steps of illumination by the unattenuated laser beam (bar beneath traces), beginning at the origin of the time axis and delivering &#x0223c;10<sup>5</sup> effective photons/s, recorded (from the left) 25, 77, and 134 min after beginning bathing the cell in 0 Ca-ASW. Between each of these spot-mode measurements of an elevation of Ca<sub>i</sub>, the cell was scanned 256&#x000d7; in the x-y scan mode by the unattenuated laser so as to uniformly excite the photoreceptor and deplete calcium stores.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8155.f12"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F13"><label>Figure 13</label><caption><p>(A) Representative receptor potentials (solid lines) and elevations of Ca<sub>i</sub> (symbols) recorded from a photoreceptor filled with fluo-4 after a flash that delivered &#x0223c;100 effective photons, when the cell was bathed for 10 min in 0 Ca-ASW (&#x02022;) and 30 min after switching the bathing solution to 0 Ca-ASW containing 100 &#x003bc;M cyclopiazonic acid (CPA&#x0003b; &#x025b5;). 5 min before the measurement in CPA, the cell was x-y scanned with the unattenuated laser beam so as to verify that the confocal spot was at the edge of the R-lobe, and to ensure depletion of Ca<sup>2+</sup> stores. The flash (bar beneath traces), 10-ms duration, 4 log<sub>10</sub> U attenuation, was delivered at the origin of the time axis. (B) Single photon signals (bumps), recorded during dim continuous illumination, when the cell was bathed for 10 min in 0 Ca-ASW, and (C) 30 min after switching the bathing solution to 0 Ca-ASW containing 100 &#x003bc;M CPA. (D) Receptor potential (solid line) and estimated Ca<sub>i</sub> (&#x02022;) recorded from a chemically bleached photoreceptor during a step of illumination by the unattenuated laser beam (bar beneath trace), beginning at the origin of the time axis and delivering &#x0223c;10<sup>8</sup> effective photons/s, recorded 45 min after switching the bathing solution to 0 Ca-ASW containing 100 &#x003bc;M CPA.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8155.f13"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F14"><label>Figure 14</label><caption><p>Mean elevation of Ca<sub>i</sub> (&#x02022;) and inward current (line) recorded from five photoreceptors filled with fluo-4 during successive steps of illumination delivered from the unattenuated laser beam. Error bars indicate SEM. To compensate for the variable latency of each response, the time, T<sub>c, nA</sub>, taken for the inward current to exceed &#x02212;2 nA was determined for each response and was subtracted from the time base of each response. (&#x025cb;) Inward current predicted by <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="FD6"/>, as described in the text.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8155.f14"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>